    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Motorbike Rental: Couples Pre-Ride Inspection</h1>
    <p>An inspection done by two is the one part of a rental where couples hold an advantage, and it should be spent deliberately. One rider checks the machine while the other photographs and remembers; one tests the brakes while the other sits aboard as ballast; one watches the lights while the other squeezes the levers. The pre-ride inspection is a routine either rider can do alone, but done together it is faster, more complete and, on a loaded two-up machine, more important than most riders assume. This guide covers it for couples.</p>
    <p>The page is part of the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> cluster, written for couples. The verified fleet prices are on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">cost hub</a>, general rental questions are on the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page, and the two-up riding technique itself is covered in the <a href="/motorbike-guides/riding-with-a-passenger-hanoi/">riding with a passenger guide</a>.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">Why two-up inspections matter more</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Tires: the loaded checks</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">Brakes: tested with two aboard</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Controls, lights and mirrors for two</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">Suspension, seat and the pillion checks</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">Documenting the machine together</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-7">The five-minute routine, split for two</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Why two-up inspections matter more</h2>
    <p>Two riders double the load, and a loaded machine is less forgiving of the defects an inspection exists to catch. A soft tire that wobbles subtly under one rider wanders clearly under two; brakes that feel adequate solo work at the edge of their margin with a passenger aboard; a tired suspension that hides behind city speeds announces itself over every bump with double the weight pressing on it. The inspection's stakes rise with the passenger, and couples should run it with that in mind.</p>
    <p>The passenger's stakes are the deeper reason. The rider chose the ride and the risk; the passenger inherited both. A couple's inspection is therefore also an act of shared responsibility, and doing it together, out loud, makes the machine a mutual decision rather than one rider's assumption. It is a small ritual with a large effect on how calm the first kilometers feel for both.</p>
    <p>The routine below follows the standard sequence, tires, brakes, controls, condition, with two-up additions at every step, and it assumes the fleet's usual machines from the light automatics to the premium scooters. The general version lives in the maintenance and inspection coverage on this site, including the <a href="/motorbike-guides/motorbike-maintenance-checklist-hanoi/">motorbike maintenance checklist</a> for what the shop's side of the arrangement looks like.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Tires: the loaded checks</h2>
    <p>Check both tires before the passenger sits, and again, briefly, after. The before checks are the standard set: firm thumb-pressure on the tread face, visible grooves across the tire's center, straight valve stems with caps, and a spun wheel watched for wobbles or a dented rim. Rental fleets keep their tires honest, but the check costs a minute and the loaded consequence justifies it twice.</p>
    <p>The loaded check is the couple's addition: with both riders aboard, at a standstill, look at the tires' squat. A properly inflated tire carries the load with a visible, modest bulge at the contact patch; a tire that flattens dramatically under two riders is underinflated for the trip you are planning, and the fix is a top-up before departure, which the shop can arrange in minutes. Ask at the counter what pressure the fleet runs, and note that a two-up week rides on the upper end of the machine's range, a question the shop answers in seconds.</p>
    <p>Split the work: one rider does the tires while the other photographs each wheel and tread, so the record and the check finish together. The division halves the time and doubles the accountability, which is the whole advantage of doing this as a pair.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Brakes: tested with two aboard</h2>
    <p>Brakes are the check where the passenger's weight matters most, and the couple's version tests the machine as it will actually ride: loaded. Start with the stationary checks, right lever firm within the first third of its travel, left lever or pedal equally firm, no sponginess, no scraping sounds, and the brake light glowing on each application. Then the roll test, at walking pace in the shop's street, with both riders aboard: each brake applied separately, feeling for progressive bite, a slight forward dip from the front, and a rear that slows without locking.</p>
    <p>The loaded roll test is the honest one. Brakes that feel adequate with one rider can arrive at the edge of their travel with two, and the difference that was invisible at the curb becomes visible at the first real stop in traffic. If the loaded test feels weak, long, or spongy, say so at the counter and ask for another machine; two-up riders should be the profile most willing to exchange a bike at handover, because the margin they ride on is the thinnest in the fleet's customer range.</p>
    <p>Split the test along the same lines as the riding: the rider brakes, the passenger watches the machine's behavior and the traffic behind. A passenger who feels a healthy, progressive brake on day one rides calmer all week, and calm is the passenger's entire contribution to two-up safety.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Controls, lights and mirrors for two</h2>
    <p>The control checks are standard: throttle snapping closed under its spring, both brake levers lighting the brake light, the headlight and its high beam, the turn signals if fitted, and a horn that sounds instantly and loudly. In Hanoi traffic the horn is a communication device first and a courtesy device second, and a weak horn on a two-up machine is a defect worth raising rather than a quirk worth accepting.</p>
    <p>Mirrors are the rider's check, adjusted with the rider seated in real posture, but couples should adjust them with the passenger's helmet on, because the rider's silhouette, and the mirror's needed angles, change with a pillion aboard. Both riders should know the machine's blind spots after adjustment: the rectangles beside and behind that the mirrors do not cover, which on a loaded bike are where the passenger's head and the luggage live.</p>
    <p>Finally the cockpit: the phone stowed or mounted with the route set, the seat latching firmly under two riders' weight, and the under-seat storage closing cleanly with the day's actual load inside. A seat that pops open over bumps with two aboard is a small defect with a large surprise factor, and the check is a ten-second push-and-latch at the curb.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Suspension, seat and the pillion checks</h2>
    <p>The suspension check is simple and two-up relevant: push down hard on the seat, front and rear, and release. The machine should compress and rebound in one smooth motion, not bounce, not crunch, and not sag. A rear suspension that wallows under a single rider's test will wander under two at speed, and the check costs ten seconds.</p>
    <p>The pillion checks belong to the passenger, and this is the inspection's most honest division of labor: the passenger tests the seat they will ride. The pillion's width and padding, the grab rail's solidity or the rider's waist as the anchor, the footpeg position and the ankle clearance from the exhaust, which deserves a hand-check, are the passenger's comfort and the passenger's call. A grab rail that flexes or a peg that folds under weight should be mentioned at the counter before the first hour becomes the first complaint.</p>
    <p>The helmet checks complete the passenger's pass: two helmets, both fitted, both fastened, both belonging to their riders rather than swapped for style. The <a href="/motorbike-guides/motorbike-helmet-guide-hanoi/">helmet guide</a> covers sizing and standards; the couple's version of the check is that each rider's helmet is adjusted to each rider's head, at the shop, before the first ride, and not on a curb halfway through the day.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Documenting the machine together</h2>
    <p>The documentation pass is where the couple's advantage is largest. One rider photographs the machine systematically, left side, right side, front, rear, wheels, seat, every existing scratch and panel mark, while the other walks the machine and points out what deserves a frame. Then swap: the pointer photographs, the photographer points. Two sets of eyes find more marks than one, and two photographers produce a complete record by accident.</p>
    <p>Photograph the loaded machine as well as the bare one, since the bike you are returning is the bike you rode, and the record should show the seat and storage as they will actually be used. Capture the odometer, the fuel state, and anything the shop writes about the machine's condition, and put the whole set in a shared album or a shared folder, the couple's filing system being one more thing couples get to build twice as fast.</p>
    <p>Repeat the full circle at return, in the same light, ideally. The before-and-after pair is what makes the hand-back a short conversation, and how deposits work generally is covered in the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/motorbike-rental-deposit-hanoi/">deposit guide</a>. The couple's version of the record is simply the same evidence, produced by two people in half the time, with the passenger's eye on the panels the rider never sees from the saddle.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">The five-minute routine, split for two</h2>
    <p>Compressed, the couple's routine is five minutes at the curb: one rider on tires and suspension, the other on lights, levers and horn; both on the loaded brake roll test; the passenger on pillion fit while the rider sets the cockpit; and the documentation circle done as a pair. The division is not bureaucracy, it is the point: two riders checking a two-up machine, in the configuration it will actually ride.</p>
    <p>Run the routine every morning, and again after any hours-long park, the same as the solo habit. The routine survives machine changes too: a couple that switches bikes mid-trip, or adds a second machine for a stretch of independent days, re-runs the same five minutes on the new machine with the same split roles. The habit is portable, the roles are stable, and the machine, whichever one it is, starts every day known.</p>
    <p>The payoff compounds across a trip. A couple whose machine is checked, loaded and documented rides with the calm that comes from having done the work, and the work, split for two, costs less than either rider notices. That is the whole argument for the couples' version of this routine: the inspection that protects two riders is the one inspection that two riders barely feel.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about couples pre-ride inspections</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How is a couples' pre-ride inspection different from a solo one?</h2>
      <p>It tests the machine as it will ride: with two aboard. The brake roll test, the tire squat check and the mirror adjustment all happen loaded, and the passenger checks their own seat, pegs, grab rail and helmet fit. The standard checks, tires, brakes, controls, lights, condition photos, are the same; the loaded versions of them are the couple's additions.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Should both riders do the inspection or just the driver?</h2>
      <p>Both, split along natural lines: the rider takes the controls, brakes and cockpit, the passenger takes the pillion checks and the documentation photos, and both do the loaded brake test together. Two sets of eyes find more, and a passenger who participates in the inspection rides calmer because the machine is a shared decision rather than an assumption.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>What should a couple do if a rental bike fails a loaded brake test?</h2>
      <p>Ask for a different machine at the counter. Brakes that feel long or spongy with two riders aboard are working at their margin exactly where two-up riding lives, and exchanging the bike at handover costs minutes while discovering the same defect in traffic costs much more. Shops expect and handle this request routinely.</p>
    </article>

